# What does "rational" mean to an economist?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why does rational mean consistent, not selfish or clever? Which choices break the rules?"
next: {"title":"How do you choose with a limited budget?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/73-how-do-you-choose-with-a-limited-budget.cast"}

## Selfish? Clever? Neither
sources:
    - id: becker1993
      title: 'Nobel Lecture: The Economic Way of Looking at Behavior'
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1993
      finding: Delivered 9 December 1992; individuals 'maximize welfare as they conceive it, whether they be selfish, altruistic, loyal, spiteful, or masochistic' (Journal of Political Economy 101(3), 1993).

