    card title "Who is rational?"

What does an economist mean by calling someone rational? Meet three people in Harbourtown, with details made up for the purpose.
    draw mia_i olav_i tom_i parallel true

B: Mia giv (@draw mia_n mia_c@)es her day-old bread away, every evening, to anyone who asks.

Olav, a fi (@draw olav_n olav_c@)sher, pays double for one brand of boat paint, though the cheap one works just as well.

B: And Tom, at Mia' (@draw tom_n tom_c1@)s counter, prefers a bagel to a muffin (@draw tom_c2@), a croissant to a bagel, and a  (@draw tom_c3@)muffin to a croissant.

B: My money's on Mia. Giving things away is the opposite of what economists assume people do.
    highlight mia

    quiz "Which of them breaks the economist's rules of rational choice?" judge false
        right: Only one of them does.
        * Mia
        * Olav
        * Tom

Only Tom  (@draw ring_tom@)breaks the rules. Mia and Olav are perfectly  (@draw tick_mia tick_olav parallel true@)rational, as far as the theory goes.

Rational do (@highlight mia@)esn't mean selfish. In his 1992 Nob (@erase ring_tom@)el lecture, Gary Becke (@draw quote@)r said people maximise welfare as they see it.

B: Whether they be selfish, altruistic, loyal, spiteful, or masochistic. His words.
    highlight quote

(@erase quote@) Nor does it mean clever. Olav's paint ma (@highlight olav@)y look like a waste to you; to the model it's simply a taste.

(@erase mia olav tick_mia tick_olav@) What rationality does ask is  (@move tom by [-640,0] duration 1.2@)consistency, and it comes down to two rules.

The first: (@draw rule1@) complete. Offered any two things, you can say which you prefer, or that you don't mind.

The seco (@draw rule2@)nd: transitive. If you prefer A to B, and B to C, you prefer A to C.

B: Tom passes the fi (@draw ok1@)rst. He has an answer for every pair. It's the sec (@draw x2a x2b@)ond he breaks: his ranking  (@highlight tom_c1 tom_c2 tom_c3@)runs in a circle.

So rational, to an econom (@draw no2@)ist, is about the shape of your choices, not their content.

## Pump the circle
vars: {"w":{"value":3,"min":0,"max":3}}
sources:
    - id: dms1955
      title: Outlines of a Formal Theory of Value, I
      authors: Donald Davidson, J. C. C. McKinsey, Patrick Suppes
      year: 1955
      finding: 'Philosophy of Science 22: 140–160; the first published money-pump example: an agent with cyclic preferences can be led to pay for a series of swaps that returns him to where he started.'

