    card title "Pump the circle"

Why should anyone care about a circle? Put Tom's three pastries on the counter (@draw muffin_l bagel_l croissant_l parallel true@), with his preferences as arrows.
    draw muffin bagel croissant parallel true

Each arrow points to the one (@draw a1@) he likes better: muffin to  (@draw a2@)bagel, bagel to croissant, a (@draw a3@)nd croissant back to muffin.

B: Say he starts (@draw ring@) with a muffin and three dollars, and will p (@draw wallet@)ay ten cents to swap for anything he likes better. You're the seller.

    ask "Tom holds the muffin. Which pastry will he pay ten cents to swap for? Tap it." choose ["bagel","croissant","muffin"] answer bagel voice b
        right: The bagel: the arrow from the muffin points to it.
        wrong: Follow the arrow that leaves the muffin: here, the bagel.

He h (@move ring to.ref bagel duration 1@)ands over the muffin a (@animate w 2.9 duration 0.6@)nd a dime, happy.

    ask "Now he holds the bagel. Which pastry do you sell him next? Tap it." choose ["bagel","croissant","muffin"] answer croissant voice b
        right: The croissant: he likes it better than the bagel.
        wrong: Follow the arrow that leaves the bagel: here, the croissant.

(@move ring to.ref croissant duration 1@) The croissant. Anoth (@animate w 2.8 duration 0.6@)er dime.

B: And now the tric (@highlight a3@)k: he prefers a muffin to a croissant.

(@move ring to.ref muffin duration 1@) Back where he started, with the same muff (@animate w 2.7 duration 0.6@)in, thirty cents poorer, and pleased at every step.

(@animate w 0 duration 3 easing linear@) Ten laps and his three dollars are gone. Economists call (@draw pump pump_by@) this a money pump.

B: So a circle isn't just untidy. It's a standing invitation to be exploited.
    flow a1 a2 a3

(@erase pump pump_by wallet@) Now change one thing: let Tom pre (@fade a3 to 0.2@)fer the croissan (@draw a3r@)t to the muffin.

His circle becomes a la (@move ring to.ref croissant duration 1@)dder, croissant on top. Offer him anything for the croissant, and he s (@draw nodeal@)ays no. The pump stops.

That's the case for tran (@highlight ring croissant@)sitivity: whoever's preferences run in a circle can be led round it forever, paying each time.

    fade fig to 0.12

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the two rules in symbols?

@math

    erase mathq

Write A, curly greater-or-equal, B for: A is at least as good as B.
    draw m_box m_line_1

Complete: for any A and B, one of the two holds, or both.
    draw m_line_2

Transitive: if A beats B and B beats C, A beats C.
    draw m_line_3

On a finite menu, those two rules are exactly what you need to give every option a number, a utility U, bigger for better.
    draw m_line_4

    erase m

@test

    fade fig to 1

    fade a3 to 0.2

    erase mathq

## Who walks into a pump?
sources:
    - id: ls1971
      title: Reversals of preference between bids and choices in gambling decisions
      authors: Sarah Lichtenstein, Paul Slovic
      year: 1971
      finding: 'Journal of Experimental Psychology 89: many subjects chose the high-probability bet (P-bet) but put a higher selling price on the high-payoff bet ($-bet); the opposite reversal was rare.'
    - id: gp1979
      title: Economic Theory of Choice and the Preference Reversal Phenomenon
      authors: David M. Grether, Charles R. Plott
      year: 1979
      finding: 'American Economic Review 69(4): set out to make preference reversals vanish with real incentives and tighter controls; the phenomenon persisted.'
    - id: chu1990
      title: 'The Subsidence of Preference Reversals in Simplified and Marketlike Experimental Settings: A Note'
      authors: Yun-Peng Chu, Ruey-Ling Chu
      year: 1990
      finding: 'American Economic Review 80(4): subjects whose inconsistent choices and prices were exploited by an actual money pump quickly stopped reversing.'
    - id: tversky1969
      title: Intransitivity of Preferences
      authors: Amos Tversky
      year: 1969
      finding: 'Psychological Review 76(1): produced systematic, predictable intransitive choices among gambles in some subjects.'
    - id: regenwetter2011
      title: Transitivity of Preferences
      authors: Michel Regenwetter, Jason Dana, Clintin P. Davis-Stober
      year: 2011
      finding: 'Psychological Review 118(1): reanalysing Tversky''s data and 20+ other studies, the choices are largely consistent with transitive preferences plus random error.'
    - id: sen1993
      title: Internal Consistency of Choice
      authors: Amartya Sen
      year: 1993
      finding: 'Econometrica 61(3): choice consistency cannot be judged without reference to the chooser''s motives; e.g. a polite guest declines the last apple but takes one when two are left.'

    box safe "Safe bet\n35/36 to win $4" #2f6b8f x 240 y 540 width 330 height 150 radius 14 font_size 36 hidden true
    box long "Long shot\n11/36 to win $16" #b5482e x 760 y 540 width 330 height 150 radius 14 font_size 36 hidden true
