    text priced "priced higher" #b5482e x 760 y 405 font_size 38 hidden true
    arrow loop_top #2f6b8f from.x 590 from.y 585 to.x 410 to.y 585 hidden true
    arrow loop_bot #b5482e from.x 410 from.y 495 to.x 590 to.y 495 hidden true
    text circle_l "a circle with money in it" #b5482e cites ["ls1971"] x 500 y 290 font_size 40 hidden true
    box ev1 "Grether & Plott, 1979: still there" cites ["gp1979"] x 500 y 280 width 640 height 80 radius 12 font_size 32 hidden true
    box ev2 "Pumped for real: they learn (1990)" cites ["chu1990"] x 500 y 280 width 640 height 80 radius 12 font_size 32 hidden true
    box ev3 "Cycles: mostly slips? (1969 vs 2011)" cites ["tversky1969","regenwetter2011"] x 500 y 280 width 640 height 80 radius 12 font_size 32 hidden true
    ellipse plate #8f887c x 420 y 330 rx 190 ry 45 hidden true
    icon apple1 size 110 set twemoji x 360 y 390 icon_look picture of "red apple" hidden true
    icon apple2 size 110 set twemoji x 480 y 390 icon_look picture of "red apple" hidden true
    text last_l "the last apple" #b5482e cites ["sen1993"] x 420 y 230 font_size 38 hidden true
    portrait sen x 820 y 330 width 190 of "Amartya Sen" hidden true
    text st1 "Reversals happen: consensus" #3d3833 x 500 y 330 font_size 42 hidden true
    text st2 "Real preferences cycle: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 250 font_size 42 hidden true
    group bets members ["safe","long","chosen","priced","loop_top","loop_bot","illus"] hidden true

    card title "Who walks into a pump?"

A pump needs a ci (@draw safe@)rcle. Do real people ever walk into one?

In 1971 the psychologists Sarah Lichtenstein and Paul (@draw long@) Slovic went looking, with pairs of bets like these.

B: (@highlight safe@) A safe bet: thirty-five chances in thirty-six of winning four dollars. A (@highlight long@) long shot: eleven in thirty-six of winning sixteen. These numbe (@draw illus@)rs are for illustration.

Asked to choose, most  (@draw chosen@)people took the safe bet. Asked what each bet was worth to them, many of the same people p (@draw priced@)ut the higher price on the long shot.

B: So the long  (@draw loop_top@)shot is worth more, yet they'd rat (@draw loop_bot@)her have the safe bet. A circle wit (@draw circle_l@)h money in it, ready to be pumped.

(@erase circle_l@) In 1979 the economists David Grether an (@draw ev1@)d Charles Plott set out to make it vanish, with real money and tighter c (@highlight loop_top loop_bot@)ontrols. It didn't.

B: But do people keep paying once someone actu (@erase ev1@)ally pumps them? In a 1990 experimen (@draw ev2@)t that did just that, they learned fast, and the reversals faded.

(@erase ev2@) Pure circles like Tom's are rarer. Amos Tversky (@draw ev3@) produced some in 1969, but a 2011 reanalysis found most such data fit  (@highlight ev3 effect underline@)consistent choosers who sometimes slip.

B: (@erase ev3@) But is every b (@fade bets to 0.15@)roken rule a mistake? Amart (@draw sen@)ya Sen's example: at dinner you take an a (@draw plate apple1 apple2@)pple from a bowl of two, but leave (@erase apple2@) the last one alone.

Your choice changed with the menu. That strains a third, quieter rule: what you p (@draw last_l@)ick shouldn't depend on what else is offered. But it isn't confusion, it's manners: the last apple is a different option.

(@erase sen plate apple1 last_l@) How sure a (@fade bets to 1@)re we? That reversals hap (@draw st1@)pen: consensus. That real preferences r (@draw st2@)un in circles: contested.

(@erase st1 st2@) So rational is a modest word: not selfish, not clever, just a ranking that hang (@highlight loop_top loop_bot@)s together, so nobody can lead you round in circles.

    quiz "Kari gives a tenth of her pay to charity every year, whatever happens. Can an economist call her rational?" intro "Four new cases." voice b
        right: Yes, if her choices are consistent: rational is about how choices fit together, not whom they serve.
        wrong: What do the two rules actually check? Here: yes, if her choices are consistent.
        * No, she isn't selfish
        + Yes, if her choices are consistent
        * Only if she gets a tax break

    quiz "Which chooser breaks transitivity?"
        right: Train, bus, car, train: the chain comes back to its start, so a seller could charge him for every step round it.
        wrong: Look for a chain that comes back to where it started: here, train, bus, car.
        * Tea over coffee now, coffee over tea in ten years
        + Train over bus, bus over car, car over train
        * Always the cheapest wine on the list

    quiz "At an auction, Siri bids more for painting A than for painting B. Owning A, she happily swaps it for B. What is this?" voice b
        right: A preference reversal: her bids rank A first, her swap ranks B first, like the long shot and the safe bet.
        wrong: Compare what her bids say with what her swap says: here, a preference reversal.
        + A preference reversal
        * Selfishness
        * Perfectly consistent choice

    quiz "Lars has two job offers. He says he can't rank them: neither is better, and they aren't equally good either. Which rule does that break?"
        right: Completeness: for any two options you must prefer one or not mind, and Lars says neither.
        wrong: Which rule asks for an answer on every pair? Here, completeness.
        * Transitivity
        + Completeness
        * Neither: he's just thinking

B: And a ranking that hangs together is exactly what you need to choose well when the money runs out.
    highlight safe long

