      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:17dhxcg'
    '|a||A pump needs a circle. Do real people ever walk into one?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5120
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||In 1971 the psychologists Sarah Lichtenstein and Paul Slovic went looking, with pairs of bets like these.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:4z6tb3'
    '|b||A safe bet: thirty-five chances in thirty-six of winning four dollars. A long shot: eleven in thirty-six of winning sixteen. These numbers are for illustration.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1iuilu6'
    '|a||Asked to choose, most people took the safe bet. Asked what each bet was worth to them, many of the same people put the higher price on the long shot.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:13h7t2w'
    '|b||So the long shot is worth more, yet they''d rather have the safe bet. A circle with money in it, ready to be pumped.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:2m1lm6'
    '|a||In 1979 the economists David Grether and Charles Plott set out to make it vanish, with real money and tighter controls. It didn''t.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1n8j3n'
    '|b||But do people keep paying once someone actually pumps them? In a 1990 experiment that did just that, they learned fast, and the reversals faded.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1ocecvt'
    '|a||Pure circles like Tom''s are rarer. Amos Tversky produced some in 1969, but a 2011 reanalysis found most such data fit consistent choosers who sometimes slip.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1r07ugv'
    '|b||But is every broken rule a mistake? Amartya Sen''s example: at dinner you take an apple from a bowl of two, but leave the last one alone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:87dx44'
    '|a||Your choice changed with the menu. That strains a third, quieter rule: what you pick shouldn''t depend on what else is offered. But it isn''t confusion, it''s manners: the last apple is a different option.':
